    {
      "ordinal": 17,
      "depth": 1,
      "kind": "rule",
      "number": "4.1",
      "title": "Historical Sources and Datation",
      "body": "Fabric aventails, often displayed over chainmail aventails, serve decorative purposes. They may feature multi-colored designs, decorative stitching, embroidery, and coats of arms. While this type of aventail is documented by sources from the 14th century, there is no evidence of fabric aventail later on. The mail present with other 15th helmets like barbutes and sallets are not aventail but mail standards/collars.\n\nThey are Approved for 14th-century and Transitional armors.\n\nThey are Denied for 15th century armors.\n\nDe Bello Civili - 1373 - Italy Weltchronik - 1385 - Germany\n\nChurch of St Georges, Schenna - 1380/1400 - Italy Runkelstein Castle, Ritten- 1395/1405 - Italy",
      "page_from": 10,
      "page_to": 10,
      "stable_key": "aventails:4.1",
      "parent_key": "aventails:4",
      "review_status": "extracted",
